<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What survives a cloud backup or a move to a new phone.

  Written as an allowlist with no exclusions, and the absence of <exclude> is the
  design rather than an omission. Under <include> semantics anything not named is
  already out, so the seven exclusions this file used to carry named paths that
  were never in scope to begin with, which is what lint reported, once per line.

  Fail-closed matters more here than the tidiness. The app's private storage holds
  an SSH key generated without a passphrase (`Environment.getSshDir`) and the
  server's connection token (`Environment.getConnectionTokenPath`), and the token
  sits inside `data/`, one directory away from the settings below. A denylist
  protects exactly the secrets someone remembered to list; naming only what may
  leave keeps the next credential file out by default, without anyone having to
  notice it was added.

  Everything omitted, and why:

  - `home/.ssh`, `home/.vscodroid/data/token`: credentials. The SSH key has no
    passphrase because that is the right trade for a phone keyboard while the key
    stays inside the app sandbox; copying it to a cloud is what breaks that trade.
  - `server`, `usr`, the toolchains: hundreds of megabytes, and first-run setup
    extracts them again from the APK anyway.
  - `external`: the projects directory. A source tree with `node_modules` in it
    would exceed any backup quota on its own, and this is the one thing users
    already keep in git.
  - `saf-mirrors`: local copies of folders the user picked through SAF. The
    originals are the documents; these are rebuilt on reopening.
  - `sharedpref`: load-bearing, and the least obvious entry on this list.
    `FirstRunSetup.isFirstRun()` answers from `setup_version` and
    `setup_version_code` in those preferences. Restore them onto a device whose `filesDir` is empty and the app
    concludes setup has already run, skips extraction entirely, and starts a
    server with no server to start. `PortFinder` keeps the port there too, which
    the workbench's IndexedDB is bound to and which means nothing on a new
    device. Leaving the domain out is what keeps a restored install honest.

  What is included is `data/Machine`, where the workbench reads its settings from
  on this side; see `Environment.getMachineSettingsPath`, which explains why it
  is that path and not the `User/` one it looks like it should be. The directory
  rather than the file, so machine-scoped state added later travels with it.
  Restoring it is worth doing because `FirstRunSetup.createDefaultSettings()`
  writes only when the file is absent, so a restored copy is kept rather than
  overwritten on the new device's first run.

  Both sections are identical on purpose. A device-to-device transfer never
  leaves the user's hands and could justify carrying more than a cloud backup
  does, but the set is small enough that the distinction would buy nothing, and
  two rules that differ are two rules to reason about every time one changes.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="file" path="home/.vscodroid/data/Machine" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="home/.vscodroid/data/Machine" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
